module Examples.Pipeline

-- Phase 7.6 integration: the first end-to-end compiler pipeline written
-- entirely in ll-lang itself. Stitches `15-moduleparser-real.lll` and
-- `16-elaborator-real.lll` into a single program: source Str -> lexer
-- -> module parser -> `List[Decl]` -> elaborator (name resolution +
-- exhaustiveness) -> list of `E002` / `E003` error strings -> printed
-- to stdout. Showcase milestone — the first time two compiler layers
-- authored in ll-lang share a single AST and run back-to-back on a real
-- source string.
--
-- The file is big (parser + elaborator in one) but mechanically
-- assembled: the parser half is copied verbatim from
-- `15-moduleparser-real.lll`; the elaborator half is adapted from
-- `16-elaborator-real.lll` to walk 15's richer `Decl` / `Expr` / `Pat`
-- AST instead of 16's minimal local AST.
--
-- Adaptation deltas vs 16:
--   * `checkExpr` handles every 15 `Expr` variant: `EInt` / `EStr` /
--     `EVar` (the only leaf that actually resolves names) / `ETagged`
--     / all four arithmetic arms / `EApp` / `ELam` / `ELetIn` / `EIf`
--     / `EMatch`.
--   * `collectDecl` / `checkDecl` / `exhaustivenessDecl` unwrap 15's
--     `DType TypeDecl` / `DFn FnDecl` / `DLet LetDecl` / `DTag Str` /
--     `DImport Str` / `DExport Decl` variants. `DExport inner`
--     recurses into the inner decl so an exported fn still registers
--     its top-level name and gets its body walked.
--   * `exhaustivenessDecl` reads the last curried param's type name
--     via `MkParam name (TR typeName)` on 15's `Param`. 15's `Pat`
--     set has no `PCon` (named constructor patterns are a later
--     slice), so `coveredCtors` is always empty — a match over a
--     declared sum type with no catch-all arm emits one `E003` per
--     constructor. This is the deliberately narrow cut that keeps the
--     integration tight.
--   * `DImport` contributes nothing to the env (real module resolution
--     is a much later phase); `DLet`'s RHS is walked now (16 skipped
--     it).
--
-- The inherited parser half still carries the Phase 7.1..7.5e doc
-- strings — kept as-is so diffing against `15-moduleparser-real.lll`
-- remains trivial.
--
-- Original 15 header follows:
--
-- Phase 7.4 / 7.5a / 7.5b / 7.5c / 7.5d: a real recursive-descent **full
-- module** parser written in ll-lang itself. Showcase milestone — the
-- first single file that can consume a whole `module M\n type ...\n
-- let ... \n fn ... = ...` source end-to-end and emit a `List[Decl]`
-- AST. Stitches together the earlier self-hosting slices:
--   * 09-lexer-real.lll       — single-pass char tokenizer
--   * 12-typeparser-real.lll  — single-line sum-type decls
--   * 13-fnparser-real.lll    — curried typed fn decls + arithmetic body
--   * 14-exprparser-real.lll  — `if-then-else`, `match`, var refs,
--                               `let-in` chains, lambdas
--
-- Phase 7.5a added `let name = expr` decls at module level and a
-- `match scrut with | pat -> body | ...` special form inside fn bodies
-- (lifting the match machinery from 14-exprparser-real.lll).
--
-- Phase 7.5b added two more expression forms inside fn bodies:
-- `\x. body` lambdas and `let name = e1 in e2` let-in chains. Both
-- dispatch from `parseExpr`, so either can nest inside any other
-- expression form the grammar already accepts. Note: `TKwLet` is now
-- ambiguous at dispatch time — `parseDecls` consumes it as a module-
-- level decl, `parseExpr` (invoked from inside an fn body) consumes it
-- as an expression let-in. Context disambiguates cleanly because the
-- two entry points are independent.
--
-- Phase 7.5c added two more items from the Phase 7.5 backlog: **string
-- literals** in fn-body expressions (`EStr s`, lexed as `TStr` via the
-- `takeStrBody` helper lifted from 14-exprparser-real.lll) and **cons
-- / nil patterns** in match arms (`PNil` for `[]`, `PCons h t` for
-- `h :: t`, right-associative via recursive `parsePat`). The lexer
-- gained `TLBrack` / `TRBrack` / `TColonColon` tokens; `parseAtom` got
-- a `TStr` arm; `parsePat` was split into `parsePrimaryPat` (which
-- handles `[]` as a primary) plus a recursive wrapper that folds a
-- trailing `:: pat` into `PCons`.
--
-- Phase 7.5d added two more items: **tagged literals** in expression
-- position (`ETagged e tag`, surface `"x"[UserId]` / `5[m]`, restricted
-- to `EInt` / `EStr` atoms to mirror the host compiler's Phase 7.2.2
-- literal-only rule) and **parametric ctor args** in type decls
-- (`TAApp head args`, bracket-form surface `Maybe[Int]` / `Result[A][E]`
-- — picked over the `(Maybe Int)` juxtaposition form because brackets
-- need no lookahead). The lexer already has `TLBrack` / `TRBrack` from
-- Phase 7.5c; `parseAtom` got a `maybeTagged` post-pass after `EInt`
-- and `EStr`; `parseTypeArgs` was split into `parseOneTypeArg` +
-- `parseBrackArgs` so bracket groups can chain and nest.
--
-- Phase 7.5e adds three more module-level decl forms — closing out the
-- last item on the Phase 7.5 umbrella: bare **`tag Name`** decls
-- (`DTag`, no body, no params), dotted **`import Foo.Bar.Baz`** decls
-- (`DImport`, path built via the same `parseModuleNameTail` helper the
-- module header uses), and an **`export`** modifier prefix that wraps
-- any existing decl in a new `DExport` variant. The lexer gains three
-- keywords `tag` / `import` / `export`; `parseDecls` grows three new
-- dispatch arms (the `export` arm reads the next decl via a new
-- `parseOneDecl` helper and wraps the result in `DExport`); the
-- pretty-printer gains three trivial arms (`tag <name>`, `import
-- <path>`, `export <show inner>`). `trait` / `impl` decls are
-- deliberately NOT in this slice — they're much more complex
-- (multi-line signatures + indented fn bodies) and live in a separate
-- Phase 7.6 slice.
--
-- Still constrained: no multi-line fn bodies, no `trait`/`impl`
-- module-level decls, no multi-line type decls, no list-literal
-- expression atoms (`[a b c]` in expression position), no tuple
-- patterns.
--
-- What IS covered is enough to prove the front-end can be a single
-- ll-lang program:
--   * module header with dot-separated TypeId segments
--   * multiple single-line sum-type decls with multi-ctor `|` chains
--   * multiple module-level `let name = expr` decls
--   * multiple single-line fn decls with curried typed params, optional
--     return type, int-literal / var-ref / arithmetic / if-then-else /
--     match-with-scrutinee / let-in / lambda / application bodies
--
-- Grammar (each decl on its own line; `\n` between decls):
--   module   = 'module' Upper ('.' Upper)* '\n' decls
--   decls    = decl*
--   decl     = typeDecl | letDecl | fnDecl | tagDecl | importDecl
--            | 'export' decl
--   tagDecl  = 'tag' Upper
--   importDecl = 'import' Upper ('.' Upper)*
--   typeDecl = 'type' Upper param* '=' ctor ('|' ctor)*
--   param    = single-uppercase-letter (A, B, ...)
--   ctor     = Upper typeArg*
--   typeArg  = Upper brackGroup*       -- Phase 7.5d: `Maybe[Int]` chain
--   brackGroup = '[' typeArg ']'       -- recursive, flattened into TAApp
--   letDecl  = 'let' lower '=' expr
--   fnDecl   = 'fn' lower paramGroup* typeName? '=' expr
--   paramGroup = '(' lower typeName ')'  |  '(' ')'
--   expr     = ifExpr | matchExpr | letInExpr | lamExpr | addSub
--   ifExpr   = 'if' expr 'then' expr 'else' expr
--   matchExpr = 'match' expr 'with' ('|' pat '->' addSub)+
--   letInExpr = 'let' lower '=' expr 'in' expr
--   lamExpr  = '\' lower '.' expr
--   pat      = primaryPat ('::' pat)?
--   primaryPat = int | lower | '_' | '[' ']'
--   addSub   = mulDiv (('+' | '-') mulDiv)*
--   mulDiv   = app (('*' | '/') app)*
--   app      = atom atom*
--   atom     = int tag? | str tag? | lower | Upper | '(' expr ')'
--   tag      = '[' Upper ']'           -- Phase 7.5d: ETagged wrapper
--
-- The pretty printer produces a deterministic, unambiguous string so the
-- runtime test can substring-match each line of the expected output.

type Maybe A = Some A | None

type Token =
  | TKwModule
  | TKwType
  | TKwFn
  | TKwIf
  | TKwElse
  | TKwLet
  | TKwIn
  | TKwMatch
  | TKwTag
  | TKwImport
  | TKwExport
  | TLower Str
  | TUpper Str
  | TInt Int
  | TStr Str
  | TLParen
  | TRParen
  | TLBrack
  | TRBrack
  | TEq
  | TBar
  | TDot
  | TBackslash
  | TArrow
  | TUnder
  | TColonColon
  | TPlus
  | TMinus
  | TStar
  | TSlash
  | TNewline
  | TEnd

-- Type-decl AST ------------------------------------------------------------

-- Phase 7.5d: `TAApp head args` is the bracket-form parametric type
-- application `Maybe[Int]` / `Result[A][E]`. Multiple bracket groups on
-- the same head flatten into one `TAApp` with a multi-element arg list.
-- Plain single-segment names still parse as `TAVar` (single letter) or
-- `TACon` (multi-char) exactly like earlier slices.
type TypeArg =
  | TAVar Str
  | TACon Str
  | TAApp Str List[TypeArg]

type Ctor = MkCtor Str List[TypeArg]

type TypeDecl = MkTypeDecl Str List[Str] List[Ctor]

-- Fn-decl + expression AST -------------------------------------------------

type TypeRef = TR Str

type Param = MkParam Str TypeRef

-- Match patterns for this slice: int literal, lowercase var binder,
-- wildcard `_`, nil `[]`, and cons `h :: t`. Phase 7.5c added `PNil` /
-- `PCons`; constructor patterns, string-literal patterns, list
-- literals, and tuple patterns still live in Phase 7.5+'s backlog.
type Pat =
  | PInt Int
  | PVar Str
  | PWild
  | PNil
  | PCons Pat Pat

-- Expression grammar for this slice: int literal, var ref (covers both
-- lowercase identifiers and uppercase constructor references — the
-- elaborator would distinguish, but for pretty-printing they're the
-- same leaf), parenthesised grouping, `+ - * /`, `if-then-else`, curried
-- juxtaposition application, AND `match scrut with | pat -> body | ...`.
-- Match arms are stored as two parallel lists inside EMatch (pats, bodies)
-- the same way 14-exprparser-real.lll does it — the current codegen
-- can't emit mutually-recursive `type MatchArm = MkArm Pat Expr`, so
-- splitting the arm into two lockstep lists dodges the issue and keeps
-- the invariant `length pats == length bodies` by construction.
type Expr =
  | EInt Int
  | EStr Str
  | EVar Str
  | ETagged Expr Str
  | EAdd Expr Expr
  | ESub Expr Expr
  | EMul Expr Expr
  | EDiv Expr Expr
  | EApp Expr Expr
  | ELam Str Expr
  | ELetIn Str Expr Expr
  | EIf Expr Expr Expr
  | EMatch Expr List[Pat] List[Expr]

-- Phase 7.5a: `let name = expr` at module level. Lives alongside type and
-- fn decls inside the top-level `Decl` sum.
type LetDecl = MkLet Str Expr

type FnDecl = MkFn Str List[Param] Maybe[TypeRef] Expr

-- Module AST ---------------------------------------------------------------

-- Phase 7.5e adds three more decl variants:
--   * `DTag name` for bare `tag UserId` decls (semantic tag
--     declaration, no body, no params);
--   * `DImport path` for `import Std.List` decls, where `path` is the
--     dot-joined qualified module name (built by walking the sequence
--     of `TUpper` / `TDot` tokens exactly like `parseModuleHeader`);
--   * `DExport inner` — a wrapper marking the inner decl as publicly
--     visible. The `export` keyword is a modifier prefix on top of any
--     existing decl (`export fn ...`, `export type ...`, `export let
--     ...`), so `DExport` just holds a nested `Decl`. Picked over an
--     `exported: Bool` field on each variant because wrapping doesn't
--     touch any existing variant shape.
type Decl =
  | DType TypeDecl
  | DLet LetDecl
  | DFn FnDecl
  | DTag Str
  | DImport Str
  | DExport Decl

type Module = MkModule Str List[Decl]

-- ---------- Char predicates ---------------------------------------------

-- ASCII uppercase / lowercase tests, same shape as 12/13/14. ll-lang lacks
-- `&&` so each predicate is two nested `if`s.
fn isUpperChar(c Char) =
  let n = charToInt c
  if n < 65
    false
  else if n > 90
    false
  else true

fn isLowerChar(c Char) =
  let n = charToInt c
  if n < 97
    false
  else if n > 122
    false
  else true

fn isIdStart(c Char) =
  if isUpperChar c
    true
  else isLowerChar c

fn isIdCont(c Char) =
  if isIdStart c
    true
  else charIsDigit c

-- ---------- Lexer --------------------------------------------------------

-- Greedy take/drop while predicate, shared shape with every earlier slice.
fn takeWhilePred(p)(cs List[Char]) =
  | c :: rest ->
    if p c
      listAppend [c] (takeWhilePred p rest)
    else []
  | _ -> []

fn dropWhilePred(p)(cs List[Char]) =
  | c :: rest ->
    if p c
      dropWhilePred p rest
    else cs
  | _ -> []

-- Unwrap `strToInt` into a raw Int; the lexer only calls this on strings
-- that are already all-digits, so the `None` branch is defensive.
fn parseIntStr(s Str) =
  match strToInt s
    | Some n -> n
    | None -> 0

-- Classify an identifier: keywords map to TKw*; uppercase-start becomes
-- TUpper (type / ctor names); lowercase-start becomes TLower (fn names,
-- params, value vars). Phase 7.5a adds `let` (module-level decls) and
-- `match` / `with` (match-as-expression inside fn bodies). Phase 7.5b
-- adds `in` (let-in chains inside fn bodies, dispatched from parseExpr).
-- Phase 7.5e adds `tag` / `import` / `export` — three new module-level
-- decl forms (a bare `tag Name` decl, a dotted `import Foo.Bar` decl,
-- and an `export` modifier prefix that wraps any existing decl in a
-- `DExport` variant).
fn classifyIdent(s Str) =
  match s
    | "module" -> TKwModule
    | "type" -> TKwType
    | "fn" -> TKwFn
    | "if" -> TKwIf
    | "else" -> TKwElse
    | "let" -> TKwLet
    | "in" -> TKwIn
    | "match" -> TKwMatch
    | "tag" -> TKwTag
    | "import" -> TKwImport
    | "export" -> TKwExport
    | _ ->
      let cs = strChars s
      match cs
        | c :: _ ->
          if isUpperChar c
            TUpper s
          else TLower s
        | _ -> TLower s

fn lexId(cs List[Char]) =
  let idChars = takeWhilePred isIdCont cs
  let leftover = dropWhilePred isIdCont cs
  let tok = classifyIdent (strFromChars idChars)
  listAppend [tok] (lexChars leftover)

fn lexNum(cs List[Char]) =
  let digits = takeWhilePred charIsDigit cs
  let leftover = dropWhilePred charIsDigit cs
  listAppend [TInt (parseIntStr (strFromChars digits))] (lexChars leftover)

-- Collect a string literal body: the opening `"` has already been
-- consumed, now greedily take chars until the closing `"`. Returns
-- (body-chars, leftover). Lifted from 14-exprparser-real.lll.
fn takeStrBody(cs List[Char]) =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '"'
      ([], rest)
    else
      let (body, leftover) = takeStrBody rest
      (c :: body, leftover)
  | _ -> ([], [])

fn lexStr(cs List[Char]) =
  let (body, leftover) = takeStrBody cs
  listAppend [TStr (strFromChars body)] (lexChars leftover)

-- Handle `:` vs `::`: peek the next char to decide. Only `::` is a
-- valid token in this grammar (cons pattern separator); a bare `:` is
-- not used anywhere, so it falls through silently.
fn lexColonOrCons(cs List[Char]) =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == ':'
      listAppend [TColonColon] (lexChars rest)
    else lexChars cs
  | _ -> [TEnd]

-- Handle `-` vs `->`: peek the next char to decide between TMinus and
-- TArrow. `->` is used by match arms (`| pat -> body`); 15 only had
-- TMinus before Phase 7.5a. Lifted from 14-exprparser-real.lll.
fn lexMinusOrArrow(cs List[Char]) =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '>'
      listAppend [TArrow] (lexChars rest)
    else listAppend [TMinus] (lexChars cs)
  | _ -> [TMinus]

-- Main loop. Newlines are significant (they separate decls); spaces and
-- tabs are skipped. Every other single-char symbol maps directly to its
-- token constructor. Phase 7.5a adds `_` → TUnder (wildcard pattern) and
-- the two-char `->` arrow via the `lexMinusOrArrow` helper. Phase 7.5b
-- adds `\` → TBackslash (lambda-intro) — `.` → TDot was already here.
fn lexChars(cs List[Char]) =
  | c :: rest ->
    if c == '\n'
      listAppend [TNewline] (lexChars rest)
    else if charIsSpace c
      lexChars rest
    else if isIdStart c
      lexId cs
    else if charIsDigit c
      lexNum cs
    else if c == '"'
      lexStr rest
    else if c == '('
      listAppend [TLParen] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == ')'
      listAppend [TRParen] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '['
      listAppend [TLBrack] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == ']'
      listAppend [TRBrack] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '='
      listAppend [TEq] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '|'
      listAppend [TBar] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '.'
      listAppend [TDot] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '\\'
      listAppend [TBackslash] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '_'
      listAppend [TUnder] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == ':'
      lexColonOrCons rest
    else if c == '+'
      listAppend [TPlus] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '-'
      lexMinusOrArrow rest
    else if c == '*'
      listAppend [TStar] (lexChars rest)
    else if c == '/'
      listAppend [TSlash] (lexChars rest)
    else lexChars rest
  | _ -> [TEnd]

fn tokenize(src Str) = lexChars (strChars src)

-- ---------- Recursive-descent parser ------------------------------------

-- Skip leading newlines between decls. Blank lines are invisible.
fn skipNewlines(toks List[Token]) =
  | TNewline :: rest -> skipNewlines rest
  | _ -> toks

-- Module name tail: `.Upper` segments. Each recursive call prepends the
-- dot + next segment to the accumulator string, then keeps walking. On
-- the first non-dot token it stops.
fn parseModuleNameTail(acc Str)(toks List[Token]) =
  | TDot :: TUpper seg :: rest ->
    let acc2 = strConcat (strConcat acc ".") seg
    parseModuleNameTail acc2 rest
  | _ -> (acc, toks)

-- `module Upper (. Upper)*`. Returns (qualified-name, remaining-tokens).
fn parseModuleHeader(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwModule :: TUpper head :: rest ->
    parseModuleNameTail head rest
  | _ -> ("?", toks)

-- Top-level driver. Reads the module header, then drives a decl loop.
fn parseModule(toks List[Token]) =
  let (name, rest) = parseModuleHeader toks
  let decls = parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)
  MkModule name decls

-- Main decl loop. Cons-pattern fast paths dispatch on the first token.
-- Anything that isn't `type`, `let`, `fn`, `tag`, `import`, or `export`
-- terminates the loop — TEnd, TNewline (already skipped), unexpected
-- tokens all fall through to the wildcard. Phase 7.5a added the `let`
-- arm; Phase 7.5e adds three more: `tag Name`, `import Foo.Bar`, and
-- the `export` modifier prefix. The `export` arm parses the remainder
-- of the token stream as any regular decl via `parseOneDecl` and wraps
-- the result in `DExport` — this keeps the modifier composable with
-- every existing decl form without duplicating the dispatch ladder.
fn parseDecls(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwType :: _ -> consTypeDecl (parseTypeDecl toks)
  | TKwLet :: _ -> consLetDecl (parseLetDecl toks)
  | TKwFn :: _ -> consFnDecl (parseFnDecl toks)
  | TKwTag :: _ -> consDecl (parseTagDecl toks)
  | TKwImport :: _ -> consDecl (parseImportDecl toks)
  | TKwExport :: rest ->
    let (inner, rest2) = parseOneDecl rest
    DExport inner :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest2)
  | _ -> []

-- Helper: turn a (TypeDecl, leftover) pair into the recursive list.
fn consTypeDecl(pair) =
  let (td, rest) = pair
  DType td :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

-- Same shape for let decls (Phase 7.5a).
fn consLetDecl(pair) =
  let (ld, rest) = pair
  DLet ld :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

fn consFnDecl(pair) =
  let (fd, rest) = pair
  DFn fd :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

-- Phase 7.5e: generic `(Decl, leftover) -> List[Decl]` cons helper for
-- parsers that already return a fully-constructed `Decl` (tag and
-- import decls build `DTag` / `DImport` directly, unlike the type /
-- let / fn paths whose helpers return the inner decl body).
fn consDecl(pair) =
  let (d, rest) = pair
  d :: parseDecls (skipNewlines rest)

-- Phase 7.5e: parse exactly one decl, returning `(Decl, leftover)`.
-- Used by the `TKwExport :: rest` arm in `parseDecls` to consume the
-- decl that follows an `export` keyword without recursing into the
-- full decl-list loop. Dispatches on the same keyword ladder as
-- `parseDecls` and returns the single parsed decl + whatever tokens
-- are left. `export export ...` falls through to a defensive `DTag
-- "?"` arm — the grammar forbids stacking, and an error there would
-- immediately surface at the next parseDecls call.
fn parseOneDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwType :: _ ->
    let (td, rest) = parseTypeDecl toks
    (DType td, rest)
  | TKwLet :: _ ->
    let (ld, rest) = parseLetDecl toks
    (DLet ld, rest)
  | TKwFn :: _ ->
    let (fd, rest) = parseFnDecl toks
    (DFn fd, rest)
  | TKwTag :: _ -> parseTagDecl toks
  | TKwImport :: _ -> parseImportDecl toks
  | _ -> (DTag "?", toks)

-- ---------- Tag-decl parser (Phase 7.5e) --------------------------------

-- Single `tag Name` decl. No body, no params — just the keyword and a
-- single TUpper. Mirrors the host compiler's KwTag arm in parseDecl.
fn parseTagDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwTag :: TUpper name :: rest -> (DTag name, rest)
  | _ -> (DTag "?", toks)

-- ---------- Import-decl parser (Phase 7.5e) -----------------------------

-- Single `import Foo.Bar.Baz` decl. The dotted tail is collected by
-- `parseModuleNameTail` — the same helper `parseModuleHeader` uses, so
-- the qualified path string is built exactly the same way on both
-- sides. Stores the path as one dot-joined string, matching how the
-- pretty printer later renders it.
fn parseImportDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwImport :: TUpper head :: rest ->
    let (path, rest2) = parseModuleNameTail head rest
    (DImport path, rest2)
  | _ -> (DImport "?", toks)

-- ---------- Let-decl parser (Phase 7.5a) --------------------------------

-- Single module-level let decl: 'let' lower '=' expr. The RHS reuses the
-- full `parseExpr` pipeline so it can be any expression the fn-body
-- grammar accepts (int lit, arithmetic, if-then-else, match, ...).
fn parseLetDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwLet :: TLower name :: TEq :: rest ->
    let (body, rest2) = parseExpr rest
    (MkLet name body, rest2)
  | _ -> (MkLet "?" (EInt 0), toks)

-- ---------- Type-decl parser (adapted from 12-typeparser-real.lll) ------

-- Single decl: 'type' Upper param* '=' ctor ('|' ctor)*
fn parseTypeDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwType :: TUpper name :: rest ->
    let (prms, rest2) = parseTypeParams rest
    let rest3 = match rest2
      | TEq :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
   
    let (ctors, rest4) = parseCtors rest3
    (MkTypeDecl name prms ctors, rest4)
  | _ -> (MkTypeDecl "?" [] [], toks)

-- Collect zero or more single-letter uppercase type params, stopping at
-- TEq (or anything else non-Upper).
fn parseTypeParams(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper s :: rest ->
    if strLen s == 1
      let (ps, rest2) = parseTypeParams rest
      (s :: ps, rest2)
    else ([], toks)
  | _ -> ([], toks)

-- First ctor is mandatory; each subsequent ctor is preceded by `|`.
fn parseCtors(toks List[Token]) =
  let (c, rest) = parseCtor toks
  parseCtorsTail [c] rest

fn parseCtorsTail(acc List[Ctor])(toks List[Token]) =
  | TBar :: rest ->
    let (c, rest2) = parseCtor rest
    parseCtorsTail (listAppend acc [c]) rest2
  | _ -> (acc, toks)

fn parseCtor(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper name :: rest ->
    let (args, rest2) = parseTypeArgs rest
    (MkCtor name args, rest2)
  | _ -> (MkCtor "?" [], toks)

-- Collect zero or more type args. Stops on TBar / TNewline / TEnd / any
-- non-Upper token. Each arg is parsed via `parseOneTypeArg`, which
-- handles both the bare form (`Int`, `A`) and the Phase 7.5d bracket-
-- form parametric application (`Maybe[Int]`, `Result[A][E]`).
fn parseTypeArgs(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper _ :: _ ->
    let (arg, rest) = parseOneTypeArg toks
    let (args, rest2) = parseTypeArgs rest
    (arg :: args, rest2)
  | _ -> ([], toks)

-- Phase 7.5d: parse a single type arg. Bare TUpper becomes `TAVar` (len
-- 1) or `TACon` (longer). If the head is immediately followed by one or
-- more bracket groups `[ typeArg ]`, those groups flatten into a single
-- `TAApp head args` ctor. Recurses through `parseBrackArgs` so a
-- bracket group's inner arg can itself be another bracket-form
-- application (`Foo[Bar[Baz]]`).
fn parseOneTypeArg(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper s :: rest ->
    let (brackArgs, rest2) = parseBrackArgs [] rest
    if listIsEmpty brackArgs
      let arg = if strLen s == 1
        TAVar s
      else TACon s
      (arg, rest2)
    else (TAApp s brackArgs, rest2)
  | _ -> (TACon "?", toks)

-- Collect successive `[ typeArg ]` bracket groups, appending each
-- parsed inner arg to the running `acc` list. Stops on the first
-- non-TLBrack token. The caller is responsible for deciding whether the
-- resulting arg list should become a `TAApp` or a plain bare form.
fn parseBrackArgs(acc List[TypeArg])(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLBrack :: rest ->
    let (inner, rest2) = parseOneTypeArg rest
    let rest3 = match rest2
      | TRBrack :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
   
    parseBrackArgs (listAppend acc [inner]) rest3
  | _ -> (acc, toks)

-- ---------- Fn-decl parser (adapted from 13-fnparser-real.lll) ----------

-- Single fn decl: 'fn' lower paramGroup* typeName? '=' expr
fn parseFnDecl(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwFn :: TLower name :: rest ->
    let (prms, rest2) = parseParamGroups rest
    let (retTy, rest3) = parseReturnType rest2
    let rest4 = match rest3
      | TEq :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest3
   
    let (body, rest5) = parseExpr rest4
    (MkFn name prms retTy body, rest5)
  | _ -> (MkFn "?" [] None (EInt 0), toks)

-- Zero or more param groups. `()` produces no params; `(name Type)` adds
-- a MkParam to the running list.
fn parseParamGroups(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLParen :: TRParen :: rest -> ([], rest)
  | TLParen :: TLower pname :: TUpper tname :: TRParen :: rest ->
    let (ps, rest2) = parseParamGroups rest
    (MkParam pname (TR tname) :: ps, rest2)
  | _ -> ([], toks)

-- Optional return type: a TUpper immediately before `=` becomes
-- Some (TR name), anything else stays None.
fn parseReturnType(toks List[Token]) =
  | TUpper tname :: rest -> (Some (TR tname), rest)
  | _ -> (None, toks)

-- ---------- Expression parser (subset of 14-exprparser-real.lll) -------

-- Decide whether a token can start an atom. Used by parseApp to know
-- when to stop collecting arguments in a juxtaposition chain. Only
-- atom-starters here: TInt, TLower, TUpper, TLParen. Newlines and
-- keywords fall through to `false` so the application layer stops
-- cleanly at decl boundaries and before `then`/`else` tokens.
fn isAtomStart(t Token) =
  | TInt _ -> true
  | TStr _ -> true
  | TLower _ -> true
  | TUpper _ -> true
  | TLParen -> true
  | _ -> false

-- Top-level expression dispatcher. Phase 7.5a added `match` as a second
-- special form alongside `if`. Phase 7.5b adds `let-in` (`TKwLet`) and
-- lambda (`TBackslash`) as two more special forms — both dispatched
-- here so they can nest freely inside any other expression. Note: the
-- top-level `parseDecls` has its own `TKwLet :: _` arm for module-level
-- `let name = expr` decls (no `in` required). At expression position,
-- the same token starts a let-in chain via `parseLetIn`. The two entry
-- points are independent so the context naturally disambiguates.
fn parseExpr(toks List[Token]) =
  | TKwIf :: rest -> parseIf rest
  | TKwMatch :: rest -> parseMatch rest
  | TKwLet :: rest -> parseLetIn rest
  | TBackslash :: rest -> parseLam rest
  | _ -> parseAddSub toks

-- `let name = e1 in e2`. Single-binder let-in. Chains are expressed by
-- nesting another `let ... in ...` in the `e2` slot (right-recursive).
-- Both `e1` and `e2` are parsed via the full `parseExpr` so any
-- expression form the fn-body grammar accepts (including nested lambdas
-- and further let-in chains) works on either side.
fn parseLetIn(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLower name :: TEq :: rest ->
    let (e1, rest2) = parseExpr rest
    let rest3 = match rest2
      | TKwIn :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
   
    let (e2, rest4) = parseExpr rest3
    (ELetIn name e1 e2, rest4)
  | _ -> (EInt 0, toks)

-- `\x. body`. Single-param lambda; multi-param is expressed by nesting
-- (`\x. \y. body`). The body is parsed at full `parseExpr` level so it
-- can contain any expression form the grammar accepts.
fn parseLam(toks List[Token]) =
  | TLower name :: TDot :: rest ->
    let (body, rest2) = parseExpr rest
    (ELam name body, rest2)
  | _ -> (EInt 0, toks)

-- `if c a else b`. `then` has been removed; condition must be a single atom.
fn parseIf(toks List[Token]) =
  let (c, rest) = parseAtom toks
  let rest2 = rest
 
  let (a, rest3) = parseExpr rest2
  let rest4 = match rest3
    | TKwElse :: r -> r
    | _ -> rest3
 
  let (b, rest5) = parseExpr rest4
  (EIf c a b, rest5)

-- `match scrut with | pat -> body | pat -> body ...`. Same shape as the
-- helper in 14-exprparser-real.lll: scrutinee is parsed via `parseExpr`
-- (it stops cleanly at `TKwWith` because `with` is not an atom-starter
-- nor an arithmetic operator), then `parseArms` pulls the two parallel
-- arm lists out of the rest of the token stream.
fn parseMatch(toks List[Token]) =
  let (scrut, rest) = parseExpr toks
  let (armLists, rest2) = parseArms rest
  let (pats, bodies) = armLists
  (EMatch scrut pats bodies, rest2)

-- Collect arms while the next token is `|`. Returns `((pats, bodies),
-- rest)` — the two arm lists stay in lockstep because each recursive
-- step cons'es onto both at once. The outer result is still a 2-tuple
-- `(result, leftover)` like every other parseX helper.
fn parseArms(toks List[Token]) =
  | TBar :: _ ->
    let (armPair, rest) = parseArm toks
    let (p, b) = armPair
    let (moreLists, rest2) = parseArms rest
    let (ps, bs) = moreLists
    ((p :: ps, b :: bs), rest2)
  | _ -> (([], []), toks)

-- Single arm. Assumes the leading `|` is still on the front. Returns
-- `((pat, body), leftover)` so the caller can destructure cleanly.
fn parseArm(toks List[Token]) =
  | TBar :: rest ->
    let (p, rest2) = parsePat rest
    let rest3 = match rest2
      | TArrow :: r -> r
      | _ -> rest2
   
    let (body, rest4) = parseArmBody rest3
    ((p, body), rest4)
  | _ -> ((PWild, EInt 0), toks)

-- Arm bodies stop at the next `|` (start of sibling arm) or at TNewline
-- / TEnd (decl boundary). Parsing at the `parseAddSub` level rather than
-- full `parseExpr` prevents an arm body from accidentally grabbing a
-- trailing `| ...` as part of a nested match — safe because 15's match
-- bodies never themselves contain bare `let`/`match`/lambdas (those are
-- Phase 7.5+).
fn parseArmBody(toks List[Token]) = parseAddSub toks

-- Primary pattern: int literal, lowercase var binder, wildcard `_`,
-- or empty-list `[]`. The recursive `parsePat` calls this once,
-- peeks for `TColonColon` to optionally fold the result into a
-- right-associative `PCons`. Ctor / list-literal / tuple / string
-- patterns all stay in Phase 7.5+.
fn parsePrimaryPat(toks List[Token]) =
  | TInt n :: rest -> (PInt n, rest)
  | TUnder :: rest -> (PWild, rest)
  | TLBrack :: TRBrack :: rest -> (PNil, rest)
  | TLower s :: rest -> (PVar s, rest)
  | _ -> (PWild, toks)

-- Pattern: a primary pattern optionally followed by `:: pat`. The
-- right side is parsed via `parsePat` itself so chains like
-- `a :: b :: rest` fold right-associatively into `PCons a (PCons b
-- (PVar "rest"))`. Phase 7.5c added `PNil` and `PCons`.
fn parsePat(toks List[Token]) =
  let (p, rest) = parsePrimaryPat toks
  match rest
    | TColonColon :: rest2 ->
      let (tail, rest3) = parsePat rest2
      (PCons p tail, rest3)
    | _ -> (p, rest)

-- Arithmetic precedence cascade, same shape as 13/14.
fn parseAddSub(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseMulDiv toks
  parseAddSubTail e rest

fn parseAddSubTail(lhs Expr)(toks List[Token]) =
  | TPlus :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseMulDiv rest
    parseAddSubTail (EAdd lhs r) rest2
  | TMinus :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseMulDiv rest
    parseAddSubTail (ESub lhs r) rest2
  | _ -> (lhs, toks)

fn parseMulDiv(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseApp toks
  parseMulDivTail e rest

fn parseMulDivTail(lhs Expr)(toks List[Token]) =
  | TStar :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseApp rest
    parseMulDivTail (EMul lhs r) rest2
  | TSlash :: rest ->
    let (r, rest2) = parseApp rest
    parseMulDivTail (EDiv lhs r) rest2
  | _ -> (lhs, toks)

-- Application layer: left-associative juxtaposition. Collects atom args
-- while the head token is an atom-starter.
fn parseApp(toks List[Token]) =
  let (e, rest) = parseAtom toks
  parseAppTail e rest

fn parseAppTail(lhs Expr)(toks List[Token]) =
  match toks
    | t :: _ ->
      if isAtomStart t
        let (arg, rest) = parseAtom toks
        parseAppTail (EApp lhs arg) rest
      else (lhs, toks)
    | _ -> (lhs, toks)

-- Atom: int, string literal, lower / upper variable reference,
-- parenthesised expr. Anything unexpected falls through to a defensive
-- `EInt 0`. Phase 7.5c added the `TStr s :: rest -> (EStr s, rest)`
-- arm; the lexer already produces `TStr` for `"..."` literals. Phase
-- 7.5d folds a trailing `[Tag]` into `ETagged` by dispatching on the
-- wider 4-token prefix `TInt/TStr :: TLBrack :: TUpper ty :: TRBrack
-- :: rest` BEFORE falling through to the plain 1-token `TInt n :: rest`
-- / `TStr s :: rest` arms. Mirrors the host compiler's Phase 7.2.2
-- rule: only int / str literal atoms can take a tag — other atom
-- shapes (`TLower`, `TUpper`, `TLParen`) never try to match a `[Tag]`
-- suffix here, so a stray `[` in those positions stays on the token
-- stream and would later error out.
fn parseAtom(toks List[Token]) =
  | TInt n :: TLBrack :: TUpper ty :: TRBrack :: rest -> (ETagged (EInt n) ty, rest)
  | TStr s :: TLBrack :: TUpper ty :: TRBrack :: rest -> (ETagged (EStr s) ty, rest)
  | TInt n :: rest -> (EInt n, rest)
  | TStr s :: rest -> (EStr s, rest)
  | TLower s :: rest -> (EVar s, rest)
  | TUpper s :: rest -> (EVar s, rest)
  | TLParen :: rest ->
    let (e, rest2) = parseExpr rest
    match rest2
      | TRParen :: rest3 -> (e, rest3)
      | _ -> (e, rest2)
  | _ -> (EInt 0, toks)

-- ---------- Pretty printer ----------------------------------------------

-- Render a TypeArg as its bare name; type vars and type cons are
-- disambiguated by the wrapping context. Phase 7.5d: `TAApp head args`
-- renders as `head[arg1][arg2]...` — each bracket group is emitted
-- via the `showBrackArgs` helper, which recursively calls `showTypeArg`
-- on every inner arg so nested applications `Foo[Bar[Baz]]` print
-- uniformly.
fn showTypeArg(a TypeArg) =
  | TAVar s -> s
  | TACon s -> s
  | TAApp head args -> strConcat head (showBrackArgs args)

-- Render a list of bracket-form args as `[a1][a2]...`. One bracket
-- group per arg, matching the surface input shape `Result[A][E]`. Lives
-- separately from `showArgs` (which renders comma-joined parens for
-- ctor-arg lists).
fn showBrackArgs(args List[TypeArg]) =
  listFold (\acc a. strConcat (strConcat acc "[") (strConcat (showTypeArg a) "]")) "" args

-- Render a list of args as `(a1, a2)` or empty string for no args.
fn showArgs(args List[TypeArg]) =
  if listIsEmpty args
    ""
  else
    let inner = listFold (\acc a. if strLen acc == 0
      showTypeArg a
    else strConcat (strConcat acc ", ") (showTypeArg a)) "" args
    strConcat (strConcat "(" inner) ")"

fn showCtor(c Ctor) =
  | MkCtor name args -> strConcat name (showArgs args)

fn showCtors(cs List[Ctor]) =
  listFold (\acc c. if strLen acc == 0
    showCtor c
  else strConcat (strConcat acc " | ") (showCtor c)) "" cs

-- Render the type-param list as ` (A) (B)` with a leading space, or empty.
fn showTypeParams(ps List[Str]) =
  listFold (\acc p. strConcat (strConcat acc " (") (strConcat p ")")) "" ps

fn showTypeDecl(d TypeDecl) =
  | MkTypeDecl name prms ctors ->
    let head = strConcat (strConcat "type " name) (showTypeParams prms)
    strConcat (strConcat head " = ") (showCtors ctors)

-- Fn-decl pretty printer, matching the shape used by 13-fnparser-real.lll.
fn showTypeRef(t TypeRef) =
  | TR s -> s

fn showParam(p Param) =
  | MkParam n t -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" n) ": ") (strConcat (showTypeRef t) ")")

fn showFnParams(ps List[Param]) =
  if listIsEmpty ps
    " ()"
  else listFold (\acc p. strConcat (strConcat acc " ") (showParam p)) "" ps

fn showReturn(r Maybe[TypeRef]) =
  match r
    | Some t -> showTypeRef t
    | None -> "?"

-- Pattern pretty-printer for match arms (Phase 7.5a + 7.5c). Phase
-- 7.5c added `PNil` (rendered as `[]`) and `PCons h t` (rendered as
-- `(h :: t)`, recursively, so chains nest as `(a :: (b :: rest))`).
fn showPat(p Pat) =
  | PInt n -> intToStr n
  | PVar s -> s
  | PWild -> "_"
  | PNil -> "[]"
  | PCons h t -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showPat h)) " :: ") (showPat t)) ")"

-- Render a single `| pat -> body` arm; helper for `showArms`.
fn showArm(p Pat)(body Expr) =
  strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "| " (showPat p)) " -> ") (showExpr body)

-- Walk the two parallel lists in lockstep to produce `| pat -> body`
-- chunks joined by single spaces. The invariant `length pats == length
-- bodies` is maintained by `parseArms`.
fn showArms(pats List[Pat])(bodies List[Expr]) =
  match pats
    | p :: ps ->
      match bodies
        | b :: bs ->
          let head = showArm p b
          let tail = showArms ps bs
          if strLen tail == 0
            head
          else strConcat (strConcat head " ") tail
        | _ -> ""
    | _ -> ""

-- Fully-parenthesised expression pretty-printer, matching 13/14's shape
-- so the runtime test can substring-match each body.
fn showExpr(e Expr) =
  | EInt n -> intToStr n
  | EStr s -> strConcat (strConcat "\"" s) "\""
  | EVar s -> s
  | ETagged inner t -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr inner)) "[") t) "])"
  | EAdd l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " + ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | ESub l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " - ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EMul l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " * ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EDiv l r -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr l)) " / ") (showExpr r)) ")"
  | EApp f x -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(" (showExpr f)) " ") (showExpr x)) ")"
  | ELam n body -> strConcat (strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "(fun " n) " -> ") (showExpr body)) ")"
  | ELetIn n e1 e2 ->
    let p1 = strConcat (strConcat "(let " n) " = "
    let p2 = strConcat (strConcat p1 (showExpr e1)) " in "
    strConcat (strConcat p2 (showExpr e2)) ")"
  | EIf c a b ->
    let p1 = strConcat "(if " (showExpr c)
    let p2 = strConcat (strConcat p1 " ") (showExpr a)
    strConcat (strConcat (strConcat p2 " else ") (showExpr b)) ")"
  | EMatch scrut pats bodies ->
    let head = strConcat "(match " (showExpr scrut)
    strConcat (strConcat (strConcat head " ") (showArms pats bodies)) ")"

fn showFnDecl(d FnDecl) =
  | MkFn name prms ret body ->
    let headPart = strConcat (strConcat "fn " name) (showFnParams prms)
    let arrowPart = strConcat (strConcat headPart " -> ") (showReturn ret)
    strConcat (strConcat arrowPart " = ") (showExpr body)

-- Let-decl pretty printer (Phase 7.5a): `let <name> = <show expr>`.
fn showLetDecl(d LetDecl) =
  | MkLet name body ->
    strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "let " name) " = ") (showExpr body)

-- Phase 7.5e: `DTag` / `DImport` / `DExport` pretty-printer arms. The
-- `DExport inner` wrapper recurses back into `showDecl` so the inner
-- decl's canonical pretty form stays untouched and the `export `
-- modifier stacks in front of any shape — `export fn ...`, `export
-- type ...`, `export let ...`, even `export tag ...` / `export import
-- ...` if the grammar ever wants it.
fn showDecl(d Decl) =
  | DType td -> showTypeDecl td
  | DLet ld -> showLetDecl ld
  | DFn fd -> showFnDecl fd
  | DTag name -> strConcat "tag " name
  | DImport path -> strConcat "import " path
  | DExport inner -> strConcat "export " (showDecl inner)

-- Render a list of decls joined by `\n`, matching the input layout.
fn showDecls(ds List[Decl]) =
  listFold (\acc d. if strLen acc == 0
    showDecl d
  else strConcat (strConcat acc "\n") (showDecl d)) "" ds

fn showModule(m Module) =
  | MkModule name decls ->
    let header = strConcat "module " name
    if listIsEmpty decls
      header
    else strConcat (strConcat header "\n") (showDecls decls)

-- ---------- Elaborator (Phase 7.6 integration) --------------------------
--
-- Name-resolution env: just a `List[Str]` of declared names. No types,
-- no source positions — this slice cares only about "is this name
-- defined?". Wrapped in `MkEnv` so future slices can carry richer data
-- (per-name types, scope depth, etc.) without changing the surface.
type Env = MkEnv List[Str]

fn envHas(env Env)(name Str) =
  match env
    | MkEnv xs ->
      listFold (\acc x. if acc
        true
      else if x == name
        true
      else false) false xs

fn envAdd(env Env)(name Str) =
  match env
    | MkEnv xs -> MkEnv (listAppend xs [name])

-- Fold a list of names into an env, extending one binder at a time.
fn envAddAll(env Env)(names List[Str]) =
  listFold (\acc n. envAdd acc n) env names

-- Collect every `PVar` binder introduced by a pattern. Wildcards, nil,
-- int-literal patterns don't bind anything. `PCons h t` binds whatever
-- the head and tail patterns bind, left-to-right. 15's `Pat` has no
-- `PCon` (named constructor patterns), so this walker only needs the
-- five variants `PInt` / `PVar` / `PWild` / `PNil` / `PCons`.
fn patBinders(p Pat) =
  | PInt _ -> []
  | PWild -> []
  | PNil -> []
  | PVar name -> [name]
  | PCons h t -> listAppend (patBinders h) (patBinders t)

-- Extract the param name from a `MkParam name (TR ty)`. Used when a
-- `DFn` extends the local env for its body walk.
fn paramName(p Param) =
  | MkParam n _ -> n

fn paramNames(ps List[Param]) =
  listFold (\acc p. listAppend acc [(paramName p)]) [] ps

-- Walk `e` under `env`, returning the list of `E002 UnboundVar <name>`
-- error strings produced by every `EVar name` not in `env` plus any
-- errors produced by sub-expressions. The only arms that actually
-- flag are `EVar`; every compound arm recurses and concatenates its
-- children's error lists. `ELetIn` / `ELam` / `EMatch` extend the
-- local env for their body sub-expression(s) before recursing.
fn checkExpr(env Env)(e Expr) =
  | EInt _ -> []
  | EStr _ -> []
  | EVar name ->
    if envHas env name
      []
    else
      let msg = strConcat "E002 UnboundVar " name
      [msg]
  | ETagged inner _ -> checkExpr env inner
  | EAdd l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | ESub l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EMul l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EDiv l r -> listAppend (checkExpr env l) (checkExpr env r)
  | EApp f x -> listAppend (checkExpr env f) (checkExpr env x)
  | ELam param body ->
    let env2 = envAdd env param
    checkExpr env2 body
  | ELetIn name rhs body ->
    let rhsErrs = checkExpr env rhs
    let env2 = envAdd env name
    let bodyErrs = checkExpr env2 body
    listAppend rhsErrs bodyErrs
  | EIf cnd thn els ->
    let ce = checkExpr env cnd
    let te = checkExpr env thn
    let ee = checkExpr env els
    listAppend (listAppend ce te) ee
  | EMatch scrut pats bodies ->
    let scrutErrs = checkExpr env scrut
    let armErrs = checkArms env pats bodies
    listAppend scrutErrs armErrs

-- Walk the two parallel arm lists in lockstep. For each arm, extend
-- the scrutinee env with every binder the pattern introduces,
-- walk the body expression under the extended env. Pattern binders
-- are only visible inside their own arm body, not across arms.
fn checkArms(env Env)(pats List[Pat])(bodies List[Expr]) =
  match pats
    | p :: ps ->
      match bodies
        | b :: bs ->
          let armEnv = envAddAll env (patBinders p)
          let armErrs = checkExpr armEnv b
          let restErrs = checkArms env ps bs
          listAppend armErrs restErrs
        | _ -> []
    | _ -> []

-- Walk a single decl under the top-level `env`. `DFn` extends `env`
-- with its own params (fn params are locals, not top-level names)
-- then walks `body`. `DLet`'s RHS is also walked — unlike 16 which
-- skipped it. `DType` / `DTag` / `DImport` have no sub-expressions.
-- `DExport inner` recurses into the inner decl so an exported fn's
-- body still gets name-resolved.
fn checkFnBody(env Env)(fd FnDecl) =
  | MkFn _ params _ body ->
    let bodyEnv = envAddAll env (paramNames params)
    checkExpr bodyEnv body

fn checkLetBody(env Env)(ld LetDecl) =
  | MkLet _ body -> checkExpr env body

fn checkDecl(env Env)(d Decl) =
  | DFn fd -> checkFnBody env fd
  | DLet ld -> checkLetBody env ld
  | DType _ -> []
  | DTag _ -> []
  | DImport _ -> []
  | DExport inner -> checkDecl env inner

fn checkDecls(env Env)(decls List[Decl]) =
  | d :: rest -> listAppend (checkDecl env d) (checkDecls env rest)
  | _ -> []

-- Collect the single top-level name each decl introduces into the env.
-- `DFn` / `DLet` / `DTag` each contribute one name; `DType` contributes
-- every ctor name (the type name itself isn't a value, matching the F#
-- host elaborator). `DImport` contributes nothing in this slice (real
-- module resolution is a later phase). `DExport inner` recurses into
-- the inner decl so `export fn foo` still registers `foo`.
fn ctorName(c Ctor) =
  | MkCtor n _ -> n

fn ctorNames(cs List[Ctor]) =
  listFold (\acc c. listAppend acc [(ctorName c)]) [] cs

fn fnName(fd FnDecl) =
  | MkFn n _ _ _ -> n

fn letName(ld LetDecl) =
  | MkLet n _ -> n

fn typeDeclCtors(td TypeDecl) =
  | MkTypeDecl _ _ ctors -> ctorNames ctors

fn collectDecl(env Env)(d Decl) =
  | DFn fd -> envAdd env (fnName fd)
  | DLet ld -> envAdd env (letName ld)
  | DType td -> envAddAll env (typeDeclCtors td)
  | DTag name -> envAdd env name
  | DImport _ -> env
  | DExport inner -> collectDecl env inner

fn collectDecls(env Env)(decls List[Decl]) =
  | d :: rest -> collectDecls (collectDecl env d) rest
  | _ -> env

-- Type -> ctor index. Build a `List[TypeCtors]` indexed by type name
-- so the exhaustiveness check can answer "what ctors does type T
-- have?". Only `DType` decls contribute; `DExport (DType td)` is
-- unwrapped one level so exported types still register their ctors.
type TypeCtors = MkTypeCtors Str List[Str]

fn typeDeclName(td TypeDecl) =
  | MkTypeDecl n _ _ -> n

fn collectTypesDecl(d Decl) =
  | DType td -> [(MkTypeCtors (typeDeclName td) (typeDeclCtors td))]
  | DExport inner -> collectTypesDecl inner
  | _ -> []

fn collectTypes(decls List[Decl]) =
  | d :: rest -> listAppend (collectTypesDecl d) (collectTypes rest)
  | _ -> []

-- Linear lookup: returns the ctor list for the given type name, or
-- the empty list if the name isn't a declared sum type.
fn lookupCtors(types List[TypeCtors])(name Str) =
  match types
    | MkTypeCtors n cs :: rest ->
      if n == name
        cs
      else lookupCtors rest name
    | _ -> []

-- A pattern counts as "catch-all relative to a sum type" if it's
-- `PWild`, `PVar`, or `PCons` — mirrors the F# host elaborator's
-- catch-all list. `PInt` / `PNil` are NOT catch-alls — they match
-- specific literals.
fn patIsCatchAll(p Pat) =
  | PWild -> true
  | PVar _ -> true
  | PCons _ _ -> true
  | _ -> false

fn armsHaveCatchAll(pats List[Pat]) =
  listFold (\acc p. if acc
    true
  else patIsCatchAll p) false pats

-- 15's `Pat` has no `PCon` (named constructor patterns), so no arm
-- pattern can ever identify a sum-type ctor. This means `coveredCtors`
-- is always the empty list — a match over a declared sum type with no
-- catch-all arm reports every constructor as missing. Deliberately
-- narrow: the integration's goal is proving the pipeline plumbing, not
-- full coverage analysis.
fn coveredCtors(pats List[Pat]) = []

-- For a given `required` ctor list and `covered` ctor list, return
-- one `E003 NonExhaustiveMatch <type> missing <ctor>` message per
-- required ctor that isn't in `covered`.
fn missingCtorErrs(ty Str)(required List[Str])(covered List[Str]) =
  match required
    | c :: rest ->
      let tail = missingCtorErrs ty rest covered
      let isCovered = listFold (\acc x. if acc
        true
      else if x == c
        true
      else false) false covered
      if isCovered
        tail
      else
        let msg = strConcat (strConcat (strConcat "E003 NonExhaustiveMatch " ty) " missing ") c
        listAppend [msg] tail
    | _ -> []

-- Extract the type name out of a param (via `MkParam _ (TR t)`).
-- `paramTypeName` returns just the string.
fn typeRefName(tr TypeRef) =
  | TR s -> s

fn paramTypeName(p Param) =
  | MkParam _ tr -> typeRefName tr

-- Extract the last param's type name from a param list. Returns "" if
-- the list is empty. Folds left so the final non-"" name wins —
-- mirrors the F# host elaborator's "clause sugar scrutinizes the
-- LAST curried param" rule.
fn lastParamTypeName(ps List[Param]) =
  listFold (\acc p. paramTypeName p) "" ps

-- Run the exhaustiveness check on a single `FnDecl` body. Shared
-- between the plain `DFn fd` arm and the `DExport (DFn fd)` unwrap.
fn exhaustivenessFn(types List[TypeCtors])(fd FnDecl) =
  | MkFn _ params _ body ->
    let lastTy = lastParamTypeName params
    if strLen lastTy == 0
      []
    else
      let required = lookupCtors types lastTy
      if listIsEmpty required
        []
      else
        match body
          | EMatch _ pats _ ->
            if armsHaveCatchAll pats
              []
            else
              let covered = coveredCtors pats
              missingCtorErrs lastTy required covered
          | _ -> []

-- Run the exhaustiveness check over a single decl. Non-`DFn` decls
-- contribute nothing. `DExport (DFn ...)` is unwrapped one level.
fn exhaustivenessDecl(types List[TypeCtors])(d Decl) =
  | DFn fd -> exhaustivenessFn types fd
  | DExport inner -> exhaustivenessDecl types inner
  | _ -> []

fn exhaustivenessCheck(types List[TypeCtors])(decls List[Decl]) =
  | d :: rest ->
    listAppend (exhaustivenessDecl types d) (exhaustivenessCheck types rest)
  | _ -> []

-- Top-level elaborator entry. First collects top-level names into a
-- fresh env AND a type-to-ctors index, then runs the name-resolution
-- pass and the exhaustiveness pass under those two indices and
-- concatenates the error lists.
fn elaborate(decls List[Decl]) =
  let env0 = MkEnv []
  let env = collectDecls env0 decls
  let types = collectTypes decls
  let nameErrs = checkDecls env decls
  let exhErrs = exhaustivenessCheck types decls
  listAppend nameErrs exhErrs

-- Render the error list as one message per line. Empty list prints a
-- single `no errors` sentinel so the runtime can tell a clean
-- elaborate apart from an elaborate that printed nothing at all.
fn showErrs(errs List[Str]) =
  if listIsEmpty errs
    "no errors"
  else listFold (\acc s. if strLen acc == 0
    s
  else strConcat (strConcat acc "\n") s) "" errs

-- ---------- Driver -------------------------------------------------------
--
-- Hardcoded ll-lang source with intentional name-resolution and
-- exhaustiveness issues. The pipeline lexes, parses, and elaborates it
-- in-memory; the expected error output is three lines:
--   E002 UnboundVar undefinedName
--   E003 NonExhaustiveMatch Shape missing Circle
--   E003 NonExhaustiveMatch Shape missing Rect
--
-- Walk-through:
--   * `type Shape = Circle | Rect` registers `Circle`, `Rect` in the
--     top-level env and in the type index.
--   * `fn good(x Int) Int = x + 1` resolves `x` locally — clean.
--   * `fn bad(x Int) Int = undefinedName` references a name that was
--     never declared — emits one `E002 UnboundVar undefinedName`.
--   * `fn shapeBad(s Shape) Int = match s with | 0 -> 1` has its last
--     param typed as `Shape`. The arm pattern is `PInt 0`, which is
--     neither a catch-all nor a named-ctor cover, so the exhaustiveness
--     pass emits one `E003` per Shape ctor (Circle, Rect).
fn main() =
  let src = "module M\ntype Shape = Circle | Rect\nfn good(x Int) Int = x + 1\nfn bad(x Int) Int = undefinedName\nfn shapeBad(s Shape) Int = match s | 0 -> 1"
  let toks = tokenize src
  let mod = parseModule toks
  match mod
    | MkModule _ decls ->
      let errs = elaborate decls
      printfn (showErrs errs)
